questions.question:
To meet a 99.99% SLA for a customer-facing REST API, the architecture team is evaluating compute options. The API serves product catalogue lookups and handles between 0 and 50,000 requests per minute depending on marketing campaigns. Traffic outside of campaigns drops to near zero for hours at a time. The team wants to avoid paying for idle capacity and needs automatic scaling without pre-provisioning. The following Lambda function configuration is proposed:

```json
{
  "FunctionName": "product-lookup",
  "Runtime": "nodejs20.x",
  "MemorySize": 512,
  "Timeout": 10,
  "Environment": {
    "Variables": {
      "TABLE_NAME": "ProductCatalogue"
    }
  }
}
```

Which characteristic of this architecture MOST directly addresses the idle-cost and automatic-scaling requirements?

The platform team manages a multi-region financial application that stores transaction records in Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL (Multi-AZ) and audit logs in Amazon DynamoDB. A compliance audit found that neither service has centrally managed backup policies, and the recovery point objective (RPO) is 1 hour. The team wants to enforce backup policies across both services from a single control plane without writing custom automation.

They draft the following AWS Backup plan configuration:

```json
{
  "BackupPlanName": "FinancialDataPlan",
  "Rules": [
    {
      "RuleName": "HourlyBackup",
      "TargetBackupVaultName": "FinancialVault",
      "ScheduleExpression": "cron(0 * * * ? *)",
      "StartWindowMinutes": 60,
      "CompletionWindowMinutes": 180,
      "Lifecycle": {
        "DeleteAfterDays": 35
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Which additional step is MOST important to ensure the backup plan applies to both the RDS instance and the DynamoDB table?

You are designing a VPC network plan for an organization that currently operates 10 workload VPCs and plans to grow to 200 VPCs over the next three years. Each VPC must have a `/24` CIDR block and must be able to communicate with every other VPC. The architecture team is reviewing the following VPC CIDR allocation for the first batch:

```json
{
  "vpc-batch-1": [
    { "name": "workload-01", "cidr": "10.0.1.0/24" },
    { "name": "workload-02", "cidr": "10.0.2.0/24" },
    { "name": "workload-03", "cidr": "10.0.3.0/24" }
  ],
  "connectivity": "Transit Gateway"
}
```

Which additional network configuration decision is MOST important to ensure this design scales to 200 VPCs without re-architecting?

To meet a 99.99% SLA for a media archive platform, the architecture team has deployed an Amazon S3 bucket that receives video files daily. Access patterns are well understood: files are frequently accessed for the first 30 days, occasionally accessed between 31 and 90 days, rarely accessed between 91 and 365 days, and must be retained for 7 years for compliance before being permanently deleted. The team applied the following lifecycle configuration but a review found it is not cost-optimized:

```json
{
  "Rules": [
    {
      "ID": "archive-rule",
      "Status": "Enabled",
      "Transitions": [
        { "Days": 90, "StorageClass": "GLACIER" }
      ],
      "Expiration": { "Days": 2555 }
    }
  ]
}
```

Which lifecycle configuration change will MOST reduce storage costs while meeting all access and retention requirements?

A retail company's DBA is reviewing the backup configuration for a production Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instance. The business requires the ability to recover the database to any point within the last 7 days with a recovery point objective (RPO) of 5 minutes. The company also needs monthly snapshots retained for 12 months for regulatory compliance. The DBA finds the following AWS Backup plan JSON applied to the instance:

```json
{
  "BackupPlanName": "rds-retail-backup",
  "Rules": [
    {
      "RuleName": "daily-backup",
      "TargetBackupVaultName": "retail-vault",
      "ScheduleExpression": "cron(0 2 * * ? *)",
      "StartWindowMinutes": 60,
      "CompletionWindowMinutes": 120,
      "Lifecycle": { "DeleteAfterDays": 7 }
    }
  ]
}
```

Which changes to the backup strategy are required to fully satisfy both the RPO and the regulatory retention requirements?

After reviewing the architecture, a solutions architect discovers that EC2 instances in private subnets are making high-volume requests to Amazon S3 to read large ML training datasets. AWS Cost Explorer shows substantial charges under the "EC2-Other" line item attributed to NAT gateway data processing fees. The current VPC has no S3-specific network path; all traffic exits through a NAT gateway in a public subnet. The architect examines the existing bucket policy:

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/MLTrainingRole"},
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::ml-training-datasets",
        "arn:aws:s3:::ml-training-datasets/*"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

The team wants to eliminate NAT gateway data processing fees for S3 traffic while keeping the bucket policy intact and without exposing the instances to the public internet.

What is the MOST cost-effective network optimization?
